# Review package: batch 6, slice sql-delta

## f11376d6 Parse the orchestrator's SQL in a worker that can be thrown away, and name a text's first fault first

Batch 6's review of the SQL check found that about 39 texts within the tool's limit, each `SELECT 1` and `+1` 9,990 times,
overflowed the parser's WASM stack and left every later call throwing `memory access out of bounds` until a restart, with no
log line. The flood's test was seen red with that very message. One library instance per process cannot be reloaded, so the
parser now lives in a worker: any failure that is not the parser's own SqlError, an error or exit of the worker, or a 10 second
deadline throws that worker away, logs one line without the text, refuses the text with kind syntax, and the next text
starts a new worker. The worker is unref'd and only a waiting caller's timer holds the process open.

The test of a real worker spinning in WASM shows the deadline frees the caller and the worker ends. A check builds sql-check.ts
with tsc under the server's own options and runs it from that output in plain Node, with a main stack larger than the worker's:
it starts its worker, keeps answering after a deep text, and the process ends by itself. Not run on the Alpine image, as
this build does not use Docker.

Also from that review: a star after an alias named like a refused function is no longer taken for a call, as the last field
itself is what counts, and the walk goes in the tree's order, so `COMMIT; SELECT pg_cancel_backend(1)` hears about COMMIT.

Cost: a typical call 0.11 ms, was 0.04; the first call and the first after a replacement about 50 ms, which the process
start no longer pays; a text that breaks the parser about 130 ms; memory stayed bounded over 310 replacements.

Ruling: the worker's source is a string run with eval, not a file - why: a file has another name under vitest, tsx and dist, and this runs the same in all three - cost if wrong: ten lines without types or lint, run by the tests and the built-output check.
Ruling: the tree crosses as JSON text - why: a structured clone recurses on a deep tree on the main stack, and JSON.parse does not - cost if wrong: a stringify per call, part of the cost above.
Ruling: the worker's stack is 2 MB, so a chain of about 3,900 operators is the new limit where about 9,600 was - why: it decides the depth on the worker's own limit, with room on both sides of the 1,000 that pass and the 9,990 refused; IN lists, AND chains and UNION chains at the tool's limit still pass - cost if wrong: a deeper text is refused and told to write it shorter.
Ruling: one text at a time, and a stuck parser holds a caller at most 10 seconds for each text ahead of it and 10 for its own - why: a text that breaks the parser must cost only itself - cost if wrong: texts in the same instant wait for each other, a fraction of a millisecond each when nothing is stuck.
Ruling: a failure of the parser now reads as a fixed message, not the runtime's - why: `Maximum call stack size exceeded` tells the orchestrator nothing it can act on - cost if wrong: only the words.
Ruling: within one statement the order is the parser's fields, not the text's; sibling statements and lists are in the text's order - why: not every node carries a location, and the finding is about sibling statements - cost if wrong: one statement with two faults of different kinds may name the later one, and it is refused either way.

